--------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE OF THIS REPOSITORY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository is VERIFICATION ONLY, by design and on purpose.

It reads a record, canonicalizes it (RFC 8785), hashes it
(SHA3-256, NIST FIPS 202), checks an Ed25519 signature (RFC 8032), and
confirms the document carries only the frozen top-level field set the
published schema defines.

All four operate on ONE artifact, held on its own. None of them consults a
second record, an index, or anything this repository does not ship.

It deliberately does NOT contain, and must not be extended to contain:

PRODUCER SIDE
  - a sealer, or any code that produces a signed record
  - authorization-token issuance, or input_hash pre-commitment binding
  - WORM commitment, S3 Object Lock sequencing, or retention derivation
  - RFC 3161 timestamp acquisition
  - any private key, or any code that signs

VERIFIER SIDE -- and this half is the one that gets added by accident
  - causality-chain traversal, or following an edge to a parent artifact
  - renewal / re-anchoring obligation checks
  - divergence adjudication
  - forward oversight-regime verification via an index

That boundary is load-bearing, and it is NOT simply "producer versus
verifier". The Apache-2.0 patent grant in section 3 reaches those claims
necessarily infringed by a contribution, alone or in combination with the
Work -- and the Work is whatever this repository contains when it is
published. Several VERIFIER-side techniques are
separately patent-pending. They are not practiced here, so they are outside
the grant today; they enter it the moment code practicing them is committed,
silently, with no licence file changed and nobody prompted to re-read this.

The safe rule: this repository verifies ONE artifact, held on its own.
Anything that needs a SECOND artifact, an index, or a renewal obligation
belongs in the private repository, or needs a deliberate decision first.

That is a correction, not a widening. The rule previously read "verifies ONE
artifact's signature, and nothing else", which stopped describing the code
when the frozen-field-set check was added in v1.0.1 -- a schema conformance
check, practicing no patent-pending technique, but more than a signature. The
line that actually carries the patent boundary is "does it need a second
artifact", because every technique listed above is a technique for relating
two or more records. Phrasing the rule that way makes it testable: the
release preflight fails on any edge traversal, parent fetch, index query or
renewal check in the payload.

